# 5. SARIT — model this properly from day one

[http://sarit.github.io/](http://sarit.github.io/)

[http://github.com/sarit/SARIT-corpus](http://github.com/sarit/SARIT-corpus)

Encoding spec:

[http://sarit.github.io/docs/sarit-guidelines.html](http://sarit.github.io/docs/sarit-guidelines.html)

Full critical-edition guidance:

[http://sarit.github.io/docs/sarit-guidelines-full.html](http://sarit.github.io/docs/sarit-guidelines-full.html)

SARIT's corpus is actual TEI P5, and its richer guidelines explicitly support variants, cross-references, people, works and controlled vocabularies. ([Sarit][3])

### Use it for

This should influence our **edition interchange model**.

```text
Atlas Work
    ↓
Edition
    ↓
SARIT TEI
    ↓
Passage
    ↓
apparatus / variants
```

We should absolutely **accept TEI as an import/export format**.

But again, don't make canonical Pāṭala storage depend on TEI XML.

Pāṭala objects → TEI adapter.

Not Pāṭala ontology = TEI.

