# 8. IIIF — MUST be native Atlas architecture

Specs:

[http://iiif.io/api/](http://iiif.io/api/)

Presentation 3:

[http://iiif.io/api/presentation/3.0/](http://iiif.io/api/presentation/3.0/)

IIIF Presentation 3 currently defines interoperable `Collection`, `Manifest`, `Canvas`, ranges and annotations over remotely hosted digital objects. ([IIIF][6])

This is enormous for Pāṭala.

A manuscript becomes:

```json
{
  "id": "PATA-MS-01882",
  "repository": "...",
  "shelfmark": "...",
  "surrogates": [{
    "type": "IIIF",
    "manifest": "https://institution/.../manifest"
  }]
}
```

Then our Atlas manuscript viewer can render:

```text
folio 24r
folio 24v
folio 25r
```

without owning those images.

And later:

```text
Pāṭala Sanskrit passage
          ↓ exact correspondence
IIIF Canvas / region
```

That is a very serious scholarly capability.

