# 16. Rethink the Atlas `Relation` object

This is probably the most important repo-level recommendation.

The old Atlas has typed/confidence-weighted relationships.

Good.

But the argument-layer work has now taught us:

> **confidence alone isn't enough.**

Atlas currently conceptually does:

```json
{
  "from": "trika",
  "to": "recognition",
  "relation": "central_concept",
  "confidence": 0.9
}
```

Future Atlas should support:

```json
{
  "id": "PATA-REL-8292",
  "subject": "PATA-TRAD-TRIKA",
  "predicate": "CENTRAL_CONCEPT",
  "object": "PATA-CONCEPT-PRATYABHIJNA",

  "assertions": [
    {
      "source": "...",
      "evidence": ["..."],
      "agent": "...",
      "method": "SCHOLARLY_RECONSTRUCTION",
      "status": "SUPPORTED",
      "review_state": "..."
    }
  ]
}
```

Because:

```text
"Abhinavagupta influenced X"
```

is scholarship.

```text
"Text A predates Text B"
```

is scholarship.

```text
"MS 182 is a witness of work X"
```

can be scholarship.

```text
"Trika absorbed Krama doctrine"
```

is definitely scholarship.

Therefore the argument/evidence architecture we're building is not a weird subsystem.

**It becomes Atlas's epistemic substrate.**

