## The architectural picture

```text
                    EXTERNAL WORLD

   PANDiT       GRETIL       SARIT       Muktabodha
      │            │           │             │
      └────────────┴───────────┴─────────────┘
                           │
                        adapters
                           │
                           ▼
              ┌────────────────────────┐
              │      PĀṬALA ATLAS      │
              │                        │
              │ canonical identities   │
              │ assertions             │
              │ provenance             │
              │ relations              │
              │ supersession           │
              │ adjudication           │
              └────────────┬───────────┘
                           │
             ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
             ↓             ↓             ↓

          scholarly      passage       argument
           graph          graph          graph

             ↓             ↓             ↓
           search       reader         debate
          timeline     translation     education
           atlas         compare        benchmark
             │             │             │
             └─────────────┼─────────────┘
                           ↓
                          API
                           ↓
                  scholars / AI / apps


AUTHORITY SIDE                     ARTIFACT SIDE

OpenAlex ─┐                         ┌─ IIIF
Crossref ─┤                         ├─ library scans
DataCite ─┼── external IDs ─ Atlas ─┤
ROR ──────┤                         ├─ manuscripts
ORCID ────┘                         └─ editions
```

And this actually reconciles two seemingly different visions we've been developing.

**Old Atlas:** “beautiful map showing how Śaiva texts, people, traditions and concepts connect.”

**New Pāṭala:** “provable Sanskrit scholarship from source span through interpretation and argument.”

They are the **same system at different zoom levels**.

The Atlas is the global graph.

Click:

```text
Abhinavagupta
```

and zoom inward:

```text
works
  → manuscripts
     → editions
        → passages
           → Sanskrit
              → translations
                 → propositions
                    → arguments
                       → scholarly disputes
```

Zoom outward:

```text
Abhinavagupta
   → teachers
   → Śaiva traditions
   → Kashmir
   → works
   → later authors
   → manuscripts
   → modern scholarship
   → institutions
```

**That's the thing I'd build.** The recent vertical-object work means we now possess something OpenAlex/PANDiT/GRETIL fundamentally don't: the ability to keep zooming *inside the document* until a graph edge resolves against actual Sanskrit evidence rather than stopping at bibliographic metadata. That should become the defining rule of Atlas V2.

